Compile-time Polymorphism:
Concept: The compiler determines which function to call at compile time before the program executes.
How it works: Achieved using Function Overloading (functions with the same name but different parameters) 
or Operator Overloading.
Key Advantages: Faster execution because the method resolution happens ahead of time.

Run-time Polymorphism:
Concept: The program determines which function to call dynamically during execution based on 
the actual object being pointed to.
How it works: Achieved using Function Overriding along with virtual functions and base class pointers.
Key Advantages: Highly flexible and supports dynamic class behavior, though it adds a slight runtime overhead due to 
virtual table (vtable) lookups.
